{
  "result": "At the edge of a small city, there was a library built inside an abandoned railway station. Its clock had stopped at 4:17, the hour when the last train departed fifty years earlier, but nobody minded. People came not to measure time, but to misplace it.\n\nEach morning, Mara, the librarian, opened the tall green doors and listened to the building wake. Pipes clicked. Pigeons negotiated in the rafters. Somewhere beneath the floorboards, an old signal bell gave a single, dignified ring. Then the first readers arrived: a baker dusted with flour, a student carrying three pens, a retired mechanic who only borrowed books about oceans.\n\nMara believed every book had a weather system. Mysteries smelled like rain on pavement. Poetry carried sudden winds. Histories were dry until opened, when they released storms of names, dates, and unfinished arguments. Children's books were bright with impossible sunlight.\n\nOne winter afternoon, a boy asked whether the station ever wished trains still came. Mara considered the silent tracks beyond the windows, silver beneath snow.\n\n\u201cPerhaps,\u201d she said. \u201cBut it has other departures now.\u201d\n\nShe showed him the travel shelves, the astronomy room, the narrow staircase leading to languages he had never heard spoken. He chose a book about islands formed by volcanoes and sat beneath the stopped clock until dusk.\n\nWhen he left, he waved goodbye as though returning from a far country. Mara locked the doors and watched his footprints cross the platform. In darkness, station seemed larger: full of journeys underway.",
